# RUBY

Ruby is *completely* object oriented - even numeric literals and values like true, false, nil are objects with invokable methods:

3.times {print "bla"}
1.upto(5) {|x| print x}

## might surprise you:

- strings are mutable ([]= to alter, << to append), use .freeze to make them imutable, avoid using strings in loops: 10.times {puts "test".object_id} -- a different object each time
- nil is false, everything else is true 
- classes (even the core built-in ones) are open, so you can exend them freely with your own methods

## exotic datatypes

- Regexp: /[Rr]uby/, /\d{5}/ 
- Range: 1..4 (includes last digit), 1...4 (excludes last digit)
Both define == operator for testing equality and === operator for testing membersihp
- symbols: :key, used as keys in hashes since strings are mutable

## punctuation in identifiers

$files      - global variable
@data       - instance variable
@@counter   - class variable
empty?      - boolean valued method
sort!       - in-place variant of sort method 
timeout=    - method invoked by assignment


